// Casino — formalized DSL (stress test).
//
// Source: https://www.pagat.com/fishing/casino.html
//
// A 2-player fishing game. No trick-taking: each turn a player plays one
// card from hand and either captures table cards (matching rank, or a
// combination of table cards summing to the played card's pip value),
// combines it with table cards into an announced "build" to capture later,
// or trails it face-up onto the table. Cards remaining on the table at the
// end of the round go to whoever captured last. Scoring: most cards (3),
// most spades (1), each ace (1), ten of diamonds (2), two of spades (1),
// plus 1 for a sweep (a capture that empties the table). First to 21 wins.
//
// STRESS-TEST FINDINGS (see structured report for full detail):
//
// - `card_value()` is hard-wired per-deck in cardlang/runtime/values.py and
//   `standard52`'s table is EMPTY — there is no in-language way to declare
//   per-rank point/pip values for a stdlib deck (the `cards: standard52 + {
//   values: {...} }` composition shown in docs/library.md is not implemented
//   by the grammar: `cards:` takes a single deck NAME, full stop). WORKAROUND
//   (forced, not stylistic): the `ranking:` declaration is repurposed purely
//   to make `rank_value()` return each numeral card's pip value. `rank_index
//   = len(ranking) - 1 - position`, so listing ranks in this exact order —
//     J Q 10 9 8 7 6 5 4 3 2 A K
//   — yields rank_value(2)=2, rank_value(3)=3, ..., rank_value(10)=10,
//   rank_value(A)=1. J/Q/K land on whatever values fall out of the ordering
//   (12, 11, 0) and are NEVER read numerically — `is_numeral(c)` excludes
//   them from every sum/build computation by comparing `c.rank` against the
//   string literals "J"/"Q"/"K" directly, matching them ONLY by rank
//   (as the real rules require for face-card captures). Casino has no
//   trick-taking, so `ranking:` carries no "trick strength" meaning here at
//   all; this is a repurposing of a construct built for a different job.
//
// - `choose` is an expression only for `choose integer in a..b`
//   (cardlang/ast/nodes.py Choose, grammar choose_expr). There is no
//   "choose a subset of a zone satisfying a joint predicate" primitive —
//   no way to ask for a set of table cards whose values sum to N in one
//   decision. Quantifiers (`any`/`all`) only bind over `player`/`team`
//   (cardlang/runtime/evaluate.py `_role_domain`), not over zone contents,
//   so "does a matching card exist" has to be spelled with the
//   `(sum over zone as c: if <pred> then 1 else 0) > 0` boolean-via-integer
//   idiom (the same idiom Cribbage's pegging loop already uses).
//
// - SIMPLIFICATION (recorded, not silently dropped): sum-capture and build
//   creation are restricted to exactly TWO cards (the played/added card plus
//   ONE table/hand card) rather than an arbitrary-size combination. This is
//   forced by the point above: exact-N subset-sum existence has to be
//   spelled out as an N-times-nested `sum over ... as ...` comprehension
//   (workable for N=2, and just barely for N=3 in `start_build`'s guard,
//   which nests hand x table x hand); a *general* N is not expressible
//   without a subset-sum stdlib primitive the language doesn't have. Real
//   Casino allows summing 3+ table cards (e.g. 2+3+4=9) and capturing
//   several disjoint groups in one play; neither is expressible here. This
//   is the game's real stress point, and it lands exactly where the task
//   predicted: sum-capture and builds are the combinatorial-table-interaction
//   the DSL was never asked to do before.
//
// - SIMPLIFICATION: builds are further restricted to at most ONE outstanding
//   build in the whole game at a time (real Casino allows several concurrent
//   builds, generally one per player). A per-card "which build is this card
//   part of" tag would need mutable per-card attributes; the language's
//   attributes mechanism (docs/decisions.md "Typed object model") is
//   documented but not implemented anywhere in the runtime (grep turns up
//   nothing outside a design comment in cardlang/types.py). A second zone
//   (`build_pile`, alongside the loose-card `table`) stands in for
//   "which cards are in a/the build" instead — this works cleanly for a
//   single build, but would need one zone per concurrent build for the
//   general case, which the language has no way to allocate dynamically
//   (no list-of-zones / dynamic zone construct).
//
// - SIMPLIFICATION (softened rule, not a silent cut): the "you may not trail
//   after building/adding to a build" forced-play rule is enforced only when
//   the builder actually has a legal capture/build alternative that turn.
//   The true rule can, in principle, leave a player holding a build they
//   cannot resolve (an opponent's `add_to_build` can change the announced
//   value out from under them) and nothing else legal — the language's
//   "no implicit actions" invariant (every decision must have >= 1 legal
//   move) demands *some* out, so trailing is allowed as a fallback exactly
//   when no capture/build move exists. This is documented here as a
//   deliberate widening of the real rule, not a bug.
//
// - The end-of-round "last capturer takes the remaining table cards" rule,
//   and the sweep bonus, are ordinary imperative statements (in the style of
//   Cribbage's pegging), not a `round` form — Casino's turn structure
//   (alternate single decisions until both hands are empty, across six
//   mini-deals) has no trick/auction/climb shape to reuse.
//
// Card attributes, `scoring_component`/`apply_components:`, and the
// `<actor> chooses <description>` expression form described in
// docs/decisions.md are all either unimplemented or narrower than the prose
// suggests; none of them are used here (see the structured report).

game Casino {

  players: 2
  direction: clockwise

  cards: standard52
  // Repurposed purely so rank_value() returns each numeral card's pip value
  // for capturing/building (A=1, 2..10=face value). See header note above.
  ranking: J Q 10 9 8 7 6 5 4 3 2 A K

  zones {
    deck             : Deck
    hand[player]     : Hand<player>
    table            : Discard          // loose (non-build) table cards, public
    build_pile       : Discard          // the single outstanding build's cards, public
    play_area        : Discard          // scratch: the card just played, before routing
    partner_area     : Discard          // scratch: the table partner(s) of a sum-capture
    captured[player] : PlayerPile<player>
  }

  state {
    score[player] : Integer = 0
  }

  phase hand_sequence repeats until (any player p: score[p] >= 21) {
    state {
      dealer        : Player  = 0
      active        : Player  = 0
      build_active  : Boolean = false
      build_value   : Integer = 0
      build_owner   : Player? = none
      last_capturer : Player? = none
    }

    before_each {
      move all cards to deck   // gather table/hands/captured/build_pile/scratch back home
      shuffle deck
      dealer        := the player where player != dealer
      build_active  := false
      build_value   := 0
      build_owner   := none
      last_capturer := none
      deal 4 cards from deck to table
      active := the player where player != dealer   // non-dealer leads
    }

    phase play {
      // Six mini-deals of 4 cards per hand (48 of the 52 cards; the other 4
      // went to the table above); players alternate single-card turns until
      // both hands empty, then the next mini-deal, until the deck runs out.
      repeat until deck is empty {
        deal 4 cards from deck to each hand
        repeat until (all player p: hand[p] is empty) {
          offer to active one of [
            trail, capture_rank, capture_sum_pair, capture_build,
            start_build, add_to_build
          ]
          active := the player where player != active
        }
      }

      // End of round: whoever captured last takes anything left on the table;
      // an unresolved build's cards go to whoever owns it.
      if table is not empty and last_capturer is not none {
        move all cards from table to captured[last_capturer]
      }
      if build_active {
        move all cards from build_pile to captured[build_owner]
      }
    }

    phase scoring {
      let count0  = sum over captured[0] as c: 1
      let count1  = sum over captured[1] as c: 1
      if count0 > count1 { score[0] += 3 }
      if count1 > count0 { score[1] += 3 }

      let spades0 = sum over captured[0] as c: if c.suit == spades then 1 else 0
      let spades1 = sum over captured[1] as c: if c.suit == spades then 1 else 0
      if spades0 > spades1 { score[0] += 1 }
      if spades1 > spades0 { score[1] += 1 }

      for each player p: score[p] += sum over captured[p] as c: if c.rank == "A" then 1 else 0
      for each player p: score[p] += sum over captured[p] as c: if (c.suit == diamonds and c.rank == "10") then 2 else 0
      for each player p: score[p] += sum over captured[p] as c: if (c.suit == spades and c.rank == "2") then 1 else 0
    }
  }

  winner: highest score
}

// === Casino-local move vocabulary ===
//
// Every turn is one `offer` over this vocabulary; each move type's `when:`
// guard narrows it to what is actually legal, and each effect embeds its own
// `chosen` card selections (the language has no per-move parameter that
// could carry "which hand card, which table partner" together — see header).

move_type trail {
  // Forced-play: the player who created/last-added-to the outstanding build
  // may not trail UNLESS no capture/build alternative exists for them
  // (see header's "softened rule" note).
  when: not must_resolve_build(actor) or not any_capture_or_build(actor)
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor] to table
  }
}

move_type capture_rank {
  when: can_capture_rank(actor)
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor]
      where c => (sum over table as t: if t.rank == c.rank then 1 else 0) > 0
      to play_area
    move all cards from table
      where t => (sum over play_area as p: if p.rank == t.rank then 1 else 0) > 0
      to captured[actor]
    move all cards from play_area to captured[actor]
    last_capturer := actor
    if table is empty { score[actor] += 1 }
  }
}

move_type capture_sum_pair {
  when: can_capture_sum_pair(actor)
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor]
      where c => is_numeral(c) and pair_sum_exists(rank_value(c))
      to play_area
    let target = sum over play_area as p: rank_value(p)
    move chosen one card from table
      where t1 => is_numeral(t1) and
        (sum over table as t2: if t2 != t1 and is_numeral(t2) and rank_value(t2) == target - rank_value(t1) then 1 else 0) > 0
      to partner_area
    let remaining = target - (sum over partner_area as p2: rank_value(p2))
    move chosen one card from table
      where t2 => is_numeral(t2) and rank_value(t2) == remaining
      to partner_area
    move all cards from play_area to captured[actor]
    move all cards from partner_area to captured[actor]
    last_capturer := actor
    if table is empty { score[actor] += 1 }
  }
}

move_type capture_build {
  when: can_capture_build(actor)
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor]
      where c => is_numeral(c) and rank_value(c) == build_value
      to captured[actor]
    move all cards from build_pile to captured[actor]
    build_active := false
    build_value  := 0
    build_owner  := none
    last_capturer := actor
    if table is empty { score[actor] += 1 }
  }
}

move_type start_build {
  when: can_start_build(actor)
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor]
      where c1 => is_numeral(c1) and
        (sum over table as t: (sum over hand[actor] as c2:
          if is_numeral(t) and is_numeral(c2) and c2 != c1
             and rank_value(c1) + rank_value(t) == rank_value(c2)
          then 1 else 0)) > 0
      to play_area
    let played_value = sum over play_area as p: rank_value(p)
    move chosen one card from table
      where t => is_numeral(t) and
        (sum over hand[actor] as c2: if is_numeral(c2) and rank_value(c2) == played_value + rank_value(t) then 1 else 0) > 0
      to build_pile
    move all cards from play_area to build_pile
    build_value  := sum over build_pile as b: rank_value(b)
    build_active := true
    build_owner  := actor
  }
}

move_type add_to_build {
  when: can_add_to_build(actor)
  effect {
    move chosen one card from hand[actor]
      where c1 => is_numeral(c1) and
        (sum over hand[actor] as c2: if is_numeral(c2) and c2 != c1 and rank_value(c2) == build_value + rank_value(c1) then 1 else 0) > 0
      to play_area
    build_value := build_value + (sum over play_area as p: rank_value(p))
    move all cards from play_area to build_pile
    build_owner := actor
  }
}

// === Named helper predicates ===
//
// Existence checks over table/hand combinations, factored out so each move
// type's `when:` guard and the `any_capture_or_build` fallback (used to
// soften the forced-play rule) name the same conditions and cannot drift
// out of step (the same discipline Stud's `can_act`/`owes`/`pending` uses).

function is_numeral(c: Card) = c.rank != "J" and c.rank != "Q" and c.rank != "K"

function pair_sum_exists(v: Integer) =
  (sum over table as c1: (sum over table as c2:
    if c1 != c2 and is_numeral(c1) and is_numeral(c2) and rank_value(c1) + rank_value(c2) == v
    then 1 else 0)) > 0

function can_capture_rank(p: Player) =
  (sum over hand[p] as c: if (sum over table as t: if t.rank == c.rank then 1 else 0) > 0 then 1 else 0) > 0

function can_capture_sum_pair(p: Player) =
  (sum over hand[p] as c: if is_numeral(c) and pair_sum_exists(rank_value(c)) then 1 else 0) > 0

function can_capture_build(p: Player) =
  build_active and (sum over hand[p] as c: if is_numeral(c) and rank_value(c) == build_value then 1 else 0) > 0

function can_start_build(p: Player) =
  not build_active and
  (sum over hand[p] as c1: (sum over table as t: (sum over hand[p] as c2:
    if is_numeral(c1) and is_numeral(t) and is_numeral(c2) and c2 != c1
       and rank_value(c1) + rank_value(t) == rank_value(c2)
    then 1 else 0))) > 0

function can_add_to_build(p: Player) =
  build_active and
  (sum over hand[p] as c1: (sum over hand[p] as c2:
    if is_numeral(c1) and is_numeral(c2) and c2 != c1
       and rank_value(c2) == build_value + rank_value(c1)
    then 1 else 0)) > 0

function must_resolve_build(p: Player) = build_active and build_owner == p

function any_capture_or_build(p: Player) =
  can_capture_rank(p) or can_capture_sum_pair(p) or can_capture_build(p)
  or can_start_build(p) or can_add_to_build(p)
